%N A401601 Irregular table read by rows: Place n circles with centers at the integer polar angles of the Archimedean spiral r = t, 1 <= t <= n, with the t-th circle having radius t: T(n,k) is the number of k-sided finite regions formed, for n >= 2, k >= 2, in the resulting graph.
%C A401601 As Pi is irrational no two circles' centers can be diagonally opposite across the origin, thus every circle will intersect every other circle in the graph. Therefore, and unlike A401600, the total number of regions, vertices and edges follow simple quadratic formulas - see Formulas below.
%C A401601 The number of regions with 2 sides, which always have one vertex at the origin, increases erratically with n, typically increasing by 1 or 2 with n but occasionally showing periods of stability, e.g., T(n,2) = 44 from n = 66 to n = 78. The most number of sides for any region in the graphs studied is 10; it is plausible this is the most number of sides possible for all n. See the attached formatted table text file and images.
